# Which efficiency matters more in the long run?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Static efficiency versus the innovation machine Why might an \"inefficient\" market grow richer?"
next: {"title":"Can we test whether markets work in a lab?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/65-can-we-test-whether-markets-work-in-a.cast"}

## Two scorecards
sources:
    - id: baumol2002
      title: 'The Free-Market Innovation Machine: Analyzing the Growth Miracle of Capitalism'
      authors: William J. Baumol
      year: 2002
      finding: In the oligopolistic sector of capitalist economies, competition is mainly fought with innovation rather than price; R&D becomes routine, an arms race among rivals.
    - id: harberger1954
      title: Monopoly and Resource Allocation
      authors: Arnold C. Harberger
      year: 1954
      finding: The deadweight loss of monopoly in US manufacturing came to about a tenth of one percent of national income.
    - id: jones2016
      title: The Facts of Economic Growth
      authors: Charles I. Jones
      year: 2016
      finding: US GDP per person has grown at a remarkably steady average of about 2 percent a year for nearly 150 years.
    - id: nordhaus2004
      title: 'Schumpeterian Profits in the American Economy: Theory and Measurement'
      authors: William D. Nordhaus
      year: 2004
      finding: Innovators in the US non-farm business sector captured about 2.2 percent of the total social surplus from innovation, 1948–2001; most benefits passed to consumers.

    pieces pieL x 230 y 410 radius 120 n 6 of sectors style.fill #f2c14e style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    text cueL "Slice it well" #b5482e x 230 y 240 font_size 38 hidden true
    shape pie1 shape circle x 555 y 410 radius 45 style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    shape pie2 shape circle x 680 y 410 radius 70 style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    shape pie3 shape circle x 855 y 410 radius 100 style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    arrow grow #2f6b8f from.x 530 from.y 285 to.x 930 to.y 285 hidden true
    text cueR "Grow it" #2f6b8f x 720 y 240 font_size 38 hidden true
    text tagA "a: the innovation machine" #2f6b8f x 720 y 600 font_size 40 hidden true
    text tagB "b: static efficiency" #b5482e x 230 y 600 font_size 40 hidden true
    text n01 "waste: 0.1%" #b5482e cites ["harberger1954"] x 230 y 165 font_size 50 hidden true
    text n2 "growth: 2%/yr" #2f6b8f cites ["jones2016"] x 720 y 165 font_size 50 hidden true
    scale share cites ["nordhaus2004"] x 120 y 250 width 760 label "Share of innovation's gains kept by innovators" unit % min 0 max 100 value 2.2 hidden true
    text big2 "2%" #2f6b8f cites ["nordhaus2004"] x 150 y 470 font_size 80 hidden true
    text inn "innovators" #2f6b8f x 150 y 400 font_size 34 hidden true
    text cons "consumers ≈98%" #3d3833 cites ["nordhaus2004"] x 580 y 440 font_size 46 hidden true
    text fee "markup: waste or fee?" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 185 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Which efficiency matters more?"

Over a life (@draw pieL@)time, which matters more: using what w (@draw cueL@)e already have p (@draw pie1 pie2 pie3@)erfectly, or  (@draw grow cueR@)inventing what we'll have next?

I'll  (@draw tagA@)argue for inventing, with William Baumol's 2002 book, The Free-Market Innovation Machine.

B: And I (@draw tagB@)'ll argue for static efficiency: every price at cost, no trade left undone, nothing wasted today.

B: Today's (@highlight pieL@) waste is certain. Tomorrow's inventions are only a promise.

Then weigh them. The best-known estimate of monopoly w (@draw n01@)aste is about a tenth of a percent of income; American income per person  (@draw n2@)has grown about two percent a year for some 150 years.

    pause 0.4

B: Growth, sure. But who says the firms c (@highlight pie3@)harging above cost caused it? Maybe they just pocket the  (@erase n01 n2@)gains. My guess: they keep half.

    erase pieL pie1 pie2 pie3 grow cueL cueR

Let's test that. Of all the gains from American innovation since 1948, what share did the innovating firms keep?
    draw share

    ask "What share of innovation's gains did the innovating firms keep? Click the line at your guess." on share store g default "50" after keep stage own
        right: About 2.2 percent, by Nordhaus's estimate: you said {g}.
        wrong: You said {g}. Nordhaus's estimate: about 2.2 percent.

William Nordhaus put it at about two  (@draw big2 inn@)percent. The other ninety-eight went to th (@draw cons@)e rest of us, as better and cheaper things.

    pause 0.5

B: Fine, inventors keep little. But a  (@highlight tagB@)price above cost still prices some buyers out today.

So the whole fight is over that markup: (@draw fee@) a waste on today's scorecard, or the fee that keep (@highlight tagA tagB@)s the machine running?

    pause 0.5

## Baumol's machine
use: payoff_matrix
with:
    row_player: Harbourtown yard
    col_player: Mainland yard
    row_strategies:
      - Design
      - Coast
    col_strategies:
      - Design
      - Coast
    payoffs:
      - [[3, 3], [6, 1]]
      - [[1, 6], [4, 4]]
    highlight_equilibrium: false
    box:
      x: 0
      'y': 190
      w: 760
      h: 380
sources:
    - id: baumol2002
      title: 'The Free-Market Innovation Machine: Analyzing the Growth Miracle of Capitalism'
      authors: William J. Baumol
      year: 2002
      finding: Oligopolistic rivals compete mainly through innovation; R&D spending becomes routine, an arms race neither side can quit; the fixed costs of innovation cannot be recovered at prices equal to marginal cost, so prices above marginal cost (and price discrimination) are needed and are not by themselves evidence of monopoly power.

    text race "arms race" #2f6b8f cites ["baumol2002"] x 885 y 470 font_size 44 hidden true
    text copy "copy overnight" #b5482e x 885 y 470 font_size 36 hidden true
    text pmc "P > MC" #8a5fa8 cites ["baumol2002"] x 885 y 470 font_size 56 hidden true
    text unit "profits, $ million a year (illustrative)" #8f887c x 370 y 165 font_size 26 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch calc center work ["MC = $40,000 a boat","R&D: $2m ÷ 100 boats = $20,000",{"tex":"P = MC + \\frac{R\\&D}{Q} = \\$60{,}000"}] font_size 34 hidden true
    mark eq_old around cell_0_0 #4f7f3a kind box hidden true

    card title "Baumol's machine"

B: Two cents in the dollar for the inventor. Then why would any firm bother inventing?
    draw grid row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1 parallel true

Baumol's answer isn't a rich monopolist funding a lab. It's rivalry among a few. Take Harbourtown (@draw row_player_label@)'s boatyard and a mainland (@draw col_player_label@) rival, with illustrative p (@draw unit@)rofits in millions a year.

If both coas (@draw cell_1_1@)t and keep selling the old boat, each makes four.

B: If my yard designs a faster hull w (@draw cell_0_1@)hile yours coasts, I take your buyers: six for me, o (@draw cell_1_0@)ne for you. And the reverse.

If both design, the new hulls can (@draw cell_0_0@)cel out, and the engineers cost money: three each.

B: So whatever you do, I'm better off desi (@highlight cell_0_1 cell_0_0@)gning. Six beats four, three beats one.

And the same holds for me. B (@draw eq_old@)oth design and earn three, though both woul (@highlight cell_1_1@)d rather coast at four.

That's Baumol's machine: among a few big rivals, research becomes a routine  (@draw race@)budget line, an arms race nobody dares to quit. And the buyers get a better hull every year.

    erase race

B: Now change one thing. Say the rival (@draw copy@) can copy any new hull overnight:  (@erase eq_old@)designing alone pays three, copying (@animate payoffs.0.1.0 3 payoffs.0.1.1 5 payoffs.1.0.0 5 payoffs.1.0.1 3 duration 2.5@) pays five.

    ask "Designing alone now pays 3 and copying pays 5. Where do the two yards end up? Tap a cell." choose ["cell_0_0","cell_0_1","cell_1_0","cell_1_1"] answer cell_1_1 voice b
        right: Both coast: coasting pays more whatever the rival does, 5 against 3, and 4 against 3.
        wrong: Whatever the other yard does, coasting now pays more, 5 against 3 and 4 against 3: here, both coast.

    mark eq_new around cell_1_1 #4f7f3a kind box

With copying free, coasting wins whatever the other yard does. The arms race stops.
    highlight cell_1_1

    pause 0.5

    erase copy

So the machine needs ideas that can be owned for a w (@erase eq_new@)hile, and  (@draw eq_old@)it needs prices that cover the engineers.
    animate payoffs.0.1.0 6 payoffs.0.1.1 1 payoffs.1.0.0 1 payoffs.1.0.1 6 duration 2

Because research is a fixed cost, every boat must sel (@draw pmc@)l above its marginal cost, or the designers are never paid. The textbook calls that gap inefficient; Baumol calls it the fee.

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the arithmetic of that gap?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade grid cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 eq_old row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1 row_player_label col_player_label unit pmc to 0.2

Say each boat costs forty thousand dollars in extra wood and work: that's its marginal cost.
    draw calc_box calc_line_1

B: The design team costs two million a year, spread over a hundred boats: twenty thousand a boat.
    draw calc_line_2

So the price must be sixty thousand: P equals MC plus research over Q. Priced at MC, the designers are never paid.
    draw calc_line_3

    erase calc

@test

    erase mathq

    fade grid cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 eq_old row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1 row_player_label col_player_label unit pmc to 1

    pause 0.3

## The bill for dynamism
sources:
    - id: bls2025
      title: Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey, annual revisions 2024 (news release, March 2025)
      authors: US Bureau of Labor Statistics
      year: 2025
      finding: Annual layoffs and discharges rose by 402,000 in 2024 to 20.2 million.
    - id: jls1993
      title: Earnings Losses of Displaced Workers
      authors: Louis S. Jacobson, Robert J. LaLonde, Daniel G. Sullivan
      year: 1993
      finding: High-tenure Pennsylvania workers displaced in the 1980s still earned about 25 percent less than expected five years after losing their jobs.
    - id: fhk2006
      title: Market Selection, Reallocation, and Restructuring in the U.S. Retail Trade Sector in the 1990s
      authors: Lucia Foster, John Haltiwanger, C. J. Krizan
      year: 2006
      finding: Virtually all labour productivity growth in US retail trade in the 1990s is accounted for by more productive entering establishments displacing much less productive exiting ones.
    - id: hk2009
      title: Misallocation and Manufacturing TFP in China and India
      authors: Chang-Tai Hsieh, Peter J. Klenow
      year: 2009
      finding: Reallocating capital and labour to equalise marginal products to the US degree would raise manufacturing TFP by 30–50% in China and 40–60% in India.
    - id: jones2016
      title: The Facts of Economic Growth
      authors: Charles I. Jones
      year: 2016
      finding: US GDP per person has grown at about 2 percent a year for nearly 150 years.
    - id: harberger1954
      title: Monopoly and Resource Allocation
      authors: Arnold C. Harberger
      year: 1954
      finding: Monopoly deadweight loss in US manufacturing about 0.1 percent of national income.

    text tagA "a: the innovation machine" #2f6b8f x 260 y 650 font_size 40 hidden true
    text tagB "b: static efficiency" #b5482e x 740 y 650 font_size 40 hidden true
    box a1 "Growth dwarfs the triangles" #2f6b8f x 260 y 535 width 390 height 90 radius 10 font_size 34 hidden true
    box a2 "Churn hurts no one for long" #2f6b8f x 260 y 420 width 390 height 90 radius 10 font_size 34 hidden true
    box b1 "Misallocation is big" #b5482e x 740 y 535 width 390 height 90 radius 10 font_size 34 hidden true
    box b2 "Less churn costs little" #b5482e x 740 y 420 width 390 height 90 radius 10 font_size 34 hidden true
    text tk_a1 "✓" #4f7f3a cites ["jones2016","harberger1954"] x 38 y 535 font_size 56 hidden true
    text tk_b1 "✓" #4f7f3a cites ["hk2009"] x 962 y 535 font_size 56 hidden true
    mark x_a2 around a2 kind strike hidden true
    mark x_b2 around b2 kind strike hidden true
    text ev1 "20 million layoffs (US, 2024)" cites ["bls2025"] x 500 y 300 font_size 42 hidden true
    text ev2 "−25% pay, five years on" cites ["jls1993"] x 500 y 300 font_size 42 hidden true
    text ev3 "retail: new stores ≈ all the gains" cites ["fhk2006"] x 500 y 300 font_size 42 hidden true
    text ev4 "China +30–50%, India +40–60%" cites ["hk2009"] x 500 y 300 font_size 42 hidden true
    arrow link #8a5fa8 from.anchor left from.ref b1 to.anchor right to.ref a1 hidden true
    text same "same churn" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 600 font_size 32 hidden true
    text st1 "Long run, growth wins: mostly agreed" #3d3833 x 500 y 310 font_size 40 hidden true
    text st2 "Worth the churn? Who pays? contested" #b5482e x 500 y 250 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "The bill for dynamism"

    draw tagA tagB a1 a2 b1 b2 parallel true

B: Granted, the machine runs on rivalry and markups (@draw tk_a1@), and its growth dwarfs the triangles. Now here's its bill.

B: Every year American employers (@draw ev1@) lay off about twenty million workers. That was 2024, a good year.

B: Most find new work, but some never recover. Long-serving Pennsylvan (@erase ev1@)ia workers lai (@draw ev2@)d off in the 1980s still earned about a quarter less five years on.

So churn does hurt, and for a long tim (@draw x_a2@)e. I strike my own claim.

    erase ev2

But stop the churn and you stop the gains. In American retail in (@draw ev3@) the 1990s, nearly all the productivity growth came from new, b (@draw x_b2@)etter stores replacing old ones.

    erase ev3

B: Then here's my strongest point. Static waste isn't (@highlight b1@) only little triangles. Hsieh and Klenow estimated that moving workers and machines to the best firms, as in Ameri (@draw ev4@)ca, would raise factory productivity by 30 to 50 percent in Chi (@draw tk_b1@)na and 40 to 60 in India.

Which is the  (@erase ev4@)twist: that moving is churn too (@draw link same@). Fixing today's waste and growing tomorrow run through the same exits and entries.

How sure are we? That over decades g (@draw st1@)rowth matters more than the triangles: mostly agreed.

B: Whether the growth is worth the lost jobs, and who should (@draw st2@) carry them: that's a question of values, and contested.

    pause 0.5

    quiz "A government caps every drug's price at the cost of making one more pill. What happens over twenty years?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: Cheaper pills now, fewer new drugs later: with price at marginal cost, nothing is left to pay for the research.
        wrong: With the price at the cost of one more pill, nothing pays for the research: here, cheaper pills now, fewer new drugs later.
        + Cheaper pills now, fewer new drugs later
        * Cheaper pills, and just as many new drugs
        * Dearer pills later

    quiz "Harbourtown's boatyard closes after a mainland rival builds better boats. What follows?"
        right: The town can gain overall while the yard's workers lose for years: buyers get better boats, but displaced workers often earn less long after.
        wrong: Buyers get better boats, while long-serving workers often earn less for years: here, the town can gain overall while the yard's workers lose.
        + The town can gain overall, while the yard's long-serving workers lose for years
        * Everyone in town gains
        * Nobody gains: a closure is pure loss

    quiz "A town bans big-box stores to protect its small shops. Going by the retail evidence, what happens to the town's retail productivity?" voice b
        right: It grows more slowly: in 1990s American retail, new, better stores replacing old ones brought nearly all the productivity growth.
        wrong: In 1990s American retail nearly all the productivity growth came from new, better stores replacing old ones: here, it grows more slowly.
        + It grows more slowly
        * It grows faster: small shops are more productive
        * No change: new stores don't matter

So which efficiency matters more in the long run? T (@highlight a1 tagA@)he growing pie, almost surely; but whether markets work also depends on how t (@highlight a2 x_a2@)hey treat the people the machine runs over.

    pause 0.5

## Where next
